// F199, item 7 of the Map/Set pairing pass: one variadic `add` over a Set and a MutableSet,
// replacing four names -- `insert(s, x)`, `set_insert_all`, `mutable_set_add` and
// `mutable_set_add_all`.
//
// This row merges where `remove` could not. Both worlds agree on what they answer -- a Set
// answers a new Set whether you add one element or five, a MutableSet answers whether anything
// was new -- so a variadic declaration subsumes the singular instead of conflicting with it.
//
// It also settles what `insert` means. The name used to do two unrelated jobs: put a value at an
// index in an array, and add a member to a set. `insert` is positional only now.
fn main() {
    val s = Set<int> { 1 }
    print(add(s, 2))
    print(add(s, 2, 3))
    print(add(s, [2, 3]))

    val ms = mutable(s)
    print(add(ms, 2))
    print(add(ms, 2))
    print(add(ms, 3, 4))
    print(snapshot(ms))

    // The ambiguity docs/DESIGN.md's `_all` paragraph guards against -- one element or two? --
    // on the collection where it is actually constructible. The checker compares the argument
    // against the *element type* first, so a lone `[int]` is one member of a `Set<[int]>` and a
    // `[[int]]` is a spread of several.
    val nested = Set<[int]> { [9] }
    print(add(nested, [1, 2]))
    print(add(nested, [[1, 2], [3]]))

    // `insert` keeps the positional meaning, and only that
    print(insert([1, 3], 1, 2))
    val b = mutable([1, 3])
    insert(b, 1, 2)
    print(snapshot(b))
}
